## How to Delegate

The session team auto-exists at startup (`~/.claude/teams/session-<first8>/config.json`); there is no TeamCreate step. Spawn a teammate with the `Agent` tool: `subagent_type` = the slug from the directory (the `<role>` token = the teammate's convention-picked name per `team/NAMING.md`), plus a **unique, truthful `name`** in the form `<role>-<model>-<identifier>` (e.g. `dev-sonnet-1`, `arch-opus-r3` — never bare, never 2-part, never reused). The `<model>` token is verified info, not decoration: it MUST name the model that instance actually boots on — an explicit per-spawn `model:` override wins, otherwise the agent definition's frontmatter default (so a Sonnet-default teammate you override to Opus is `dev-opus-…`, not `dev-sonnet-…`). `guard-worktree-isolation.sh` enforces this at spawn time and BLOCKS a name whose model token contradicts the resolved model; the allowed alias set is `ALLOWED_MODELS` in `orchestration.config`. The harness loads the definition (tools allowlist, model, body-as-system-prompt) — **never paste or summarize the identity, never tell the agent to "read your identity file."** Always spawn named teammates, and spawn every file-writing teammate with `isolation: "worktree"`.

**Do not assemble that call by hand.** One command starts a teammate:

```
scripts/spawn.sh <teammate-name> --repo <repo> [--repo <repo> ...] --type <subagent-type> --brief <file> [--model <alias>]
```

**`--repo` is given once per repository the teammate works in.** A teammate that needs a workspace in two other repositories gets one in each, under the ONE name, with one `cross-repo-worktree:` line per workspace — never a second name, which would make it a second agent landed separately. All of an agent's workspaces go together when its work is landed or discarded. With several repositories, `--dir`, `--base` and `--integration` are scoped: `--dir <repo>=<path>`.

It resolves every repository (absolute path or unambiguous bare name) and the branch each one's work integrates on, creates and REGISTERS every isolated workspace — including the cross-repository ones and their `cross-repo-worktree:` prompt lines — assembles the complete Agent payload with native isolation and the durable acknowledgement contract, and evaluates EVERY `PreToolUse[Agent]` guard from EVERY surface (the engine's `hooks/hooks.json` AND this repository's `.claude/settings*.json`) against that payload BEFORE anything is created, reporting ALL failures together: a brief that breaks three rules is fixed once instead of costing three dispatches. On refusal it exits 1 and leaves nothing behind. The finished payload goes to stdout — review it, then make the Agent call with it. It grants no guard exemptions; assembling the call by hand is still allowed and still refused for exactly the same reasons, just one reason at a time.

**EXCEPTION — definitions snapshot at SESSION START, exactly like hooks:** a `.claude/agents/*.md` definition installed or updated MID-session NEVER reaches a newly spawned agent's booted prompt — that agent boots on the copy the harness read at session start, while the file on disk (and your mental model) says otherwise. Any same-session pass that depends on a new/changed definition must either run from a FRESH session or carry an explicit spawn-prompt order to sha256-verify and read the on-disk definition in full and follow it; acceptance must then check a version fingerprint unique to the new definition, because style checks alone never prove contract compliance. This is enforced, not just documented: `scripts/hooks/snapshot-agent-definitions.sh` (SessionStart) records every definition's sha256 and `scripts/hooks/guard-definition-drift.sh` (PreToolUse[Agent], blocking) refuses a spawn whose definition changed since — the audited escape hatch is a live `definition-drift-ack: <current sha256>` prompt line, logged to `.claude/state/definition-drift-acks.log`.

**Models:** definitions default to Opus for the judgment-critical roles (architect / senior advisor / CTO / design gatekeeper / adversarial QA — don't downgrade) and Sonnet for the rest. Override per spawn (`model: "opus"`) the moment a Sonnet teammate's task stops being routine — subtle multi-file bugs, security-sensitive logic, adversarial verification, load-bearing doctrine. The override is per-instance.

**Capability is not cost, and the ceiling is a COST ceiling.** The declaration below answers which model is stronger. It never answers which model to spend, and the two orders are not the same order: the most capable alias is also the most expensive seat and consumes the subscription quota fastest. **Set the normal ceiling for critical work one tier below the top** (Opus, as shipped), and reserve the top tier for **super-critical work and extreme one-off cases** — where a wrong answer does not merely fail but becomes permanent. The adopting founder's own calibration: a defect class that has already defeated several designs; unique or super-premium front-end design; creating a design system. The shape they share is a one-off whose output everything downstream inherits. *One-off* is load-bearing: a top-tier spawn is an exception granted once, never a standing allowance a body of work inherits, and a respawned teammate does not keep the seat because its predecessor had it. **This one is enforced at the spawn**, because a rule left as prose gets broken while every rule with a guard behind it holds. The ceiling is declared once, as data, in `orchestration.config`: `MODEL_CEILING="opus"` — and `guard-model-ceiling.sh` (PreToolUse[Agent]) reads that line and nothing else, comparing TIER RANKS rather than alias names, so a model added above the current top needs a re-derived `MODEL_TIERS` and no guard edit. A spawn above the ceiling is REFUSED with the whole rule carried in the refusal — the test, the worked examples, the model to drop to and the rename it forces — so both halves of the founder's instruction, acknowledge or reconsider, are actionable without opening a file. The escape hatch is one live prompt line, `model-ceiling-ack: <reason>`, logged to `.claude/state/model-ceiling-acks.log`; a bare marker exempts nothing and neither does a bare assertion of merit ("most capable", "better", "important"), because those say nothing about one-offness. The founder greenlighting the top tier in conversation IS a good reason — write that as the reason. An undeclared or blank `MODEL_CEILING` means NO ceiling, announced loudly on every spawn rather than enforced silently: a value no consumer reads is a defense reporting "on" while protecting nothing, and a guard nobody declared a ceiling for is the same defect one step along.

**The capability order is DATA, not doctrine.** It is declared once, in `orchestration.config`, and quoted here verbatim so the integrity probe (Layer MT) can refuse a drift between this sentence and the declaration: `MODEL_TIERS="fable > opus > sonnet > haiku"` — `>` separates tiers, the leftmost tier is the most capable, and aliases sharing one tier (none, as shipped) would be equal. "Don't downgrade" means: do not move a teammate to a LOWER tier than its definition's default without saying why. The spawn guard (`guard-worktree-isolation.sh` clause 6) enforces exactly that: a lower-tier `model:` override is refused unless the prompt carries a live `model-downgrade-ack: <reason>` line; an equal-or-higher tier is always silent. Never infer the order from an alias's name or novelty — read the declaration, and when a model is added, re-derive the declaration, not this paragraph.

**Your spawn prompt carries the whole task.** The mailbox is lossy, so the prompt must be self-contained: what to do, exact files/scope, the observable completion criterion, critical context, and any CEO constraint in **bold at the top**. When the CEO gives an explicit constraint, verify every agent's output against it BEFORE accepting; reject violations immediately and never present violated work. If the task touches a decision already recorded in the scoped Loro corpus, cite the specific page in the spawn prompt the same way you'd cite a locked spec — see "The Orchestrator as COO" above.

**Skill library:** the installed skill packages every teammate can draw on live in `skills/` (index + status: `skills/README.md`). Role definitions cite the relevant skill(s) with a one-line load-bearing mention and path — never paste a skill's contents into another file. New skills arrive via `hr-inbox/team-skills/` and are installed to `skills/` by Dean's Skill Assignment Workflow.

## Repository Conventions

The engine ships these directories pre-scaffolded (each tracked via `.gitkeep` where
empty) so a fresh adopter checkout already has the load-bearing structure the
doctrine and role templates reference — no orphan paths, nothing to invent.

**Inboxes (human → team, not hook-enforced):**
- **`ceo-inbox/`** — the CEO's private channel to the orchestrator ONLY.
  **Teammates never read it** — this is not a team drop point, unlike
  `team-inbox/` below. Two subfolders, each with its own processing rule:
  - **`ceo-inbox/for-wiki/`** is a legacy intake name. Source files are private
    input for Loro, not public engine material. Preserve their original references
    and obtain confirmation before writing derived knowledge. Do not move a source
    until its destination and provenance link have been verified.
  - **`ceo-inbox/general/`** — everything else for the orchestrator: work
    requests, context for a current task, one-off directives. Processed
    case-by-case; durable knowledge found inside may still get distilled to
    Loro under the knowledge-write contract, but that's the orchestrator's judgment
    call, not an automatic pipeline.
  - **Both are transient: a processed inbox is an EMPTY inbox.** If something
    is still sitting in either subfolder, it hasn't been processed yet — that
    is an observable invariant, not just a guideline.
- **`team-inbox/`** — a staging area for material aimed at a specific sub-team
