     A dialect line with no format line is half a rule: "color" and "31/12/2026"
     on the same screen is still the wrong locale. -->

**Vocabulary is part of the product, not a coat of paint.** When a name is wrong for the audience, the fix is a rename with a stated migration, not a synonym in the docs — see `UPGRADING.md` for the worked example of one this engine did to itself.

**And it is ENFORCED at the write, because a written-down dialect rule decays on a measured schedule.** This one was ruled on, swept across 654 sites — and roughly twenty fresh violations went into the same record within HOURS, including into the page that carries the ruling. The sweep cleaned what existed and constrained nothing that came after, which is the same shape as every rule in this file that has no chokepoint behind it. So set **`DIALECT_TARGET`** in `orchestration.config` and `scripts/hooks/dialect-guard.sh` fixes the word in the write where the fix is mechanical (prose, comments, sentences in code) and tells the model what it changed, and refuses the write where it is not (identifiers, one-word string literals, markup and config, quotations, names), naming the word and the fix. Blank it and the guard is off, silently — nothing here imposes American English on a product that does not speak it. The vocabulary is data in `scripts/lib/dialect/dialect-en-US.generated.dict`, and probe **Layer T** hashes it and the fixer, because a hash-matched guard over an emptied word list is a green tick over nothing. Quoted external material, code, identifiers, captured evidence and vendor legal text are exempt by construction; anything else is exempt only by DECLARING it — `dialect-exempt: <reason>`, on the line, where a reviewer sees it.

