### Git Worktree Isolation

**One agent = one worktree, via native `isolation: "worktree"`.** Native worktrees land at `.claude/worktrees/agent-<id>/` on a `worktree-<id>` branch, branched from local HEAD. A PreToolUse hook (`scripts/hooks/guard-main-checkout-writes.sh`) blocks main-checkout writes to your protected source trees (configured as `PROTECTED_PATHS` in `orchestration.config`) — all source reaches main only via `git merge`. A companion PreToolUse[Bash] hook (`scripts/hooks/guard-bash-main-writes.sh`) closes the raw-Bash gap: a compound `cd <root> && mkdir/cp/rm <tree>/…` or absolute-path write into a protected tree — which the Write/Edit guard never sees — is auto-denied so it never becomes an interactive permission prompt to you. Engineer workflow skill: `skills/using-git-worktrees/SKILL.md`.

Every file-writing teammate spawn requires an isolated worktree — enforced structurally by `scripts/hooks/guard-worktree-isolation.sh` (PreToolUse[Agent], blocking) with `scripts/hooks/detect-nonnative-worktree.sh` as its non-blocking PostToolUse backstop. **All** file-writing teammates run in isolated worktrees, including any device/visual QA (the install/verify scripts resolve the true main checkout from any location via `scripts/lib/resolve-main-checkout.sh`). Read-only agent types are exempt via `READONLY_ALLOWLIST` in `orchestration.config`. The well-formed, truthful `<role>-<model>-<identifier>` name requirement is never relaxed.

**Never resume a landed/cleaned agent for file work — spawn fresh.** A `SendMessage` to a completed teammate RESUMES it from its transcript, and `guard-worktree-isolation.sh` never fires on a resume (it only sees spawns); if that agent's worktree was already landed and removed, it wakes with no isolated workspace and improvises (main-checkout writes / lost work). `scripts/hooks/guard-resume-isolation.sh` (PreToolUse[SendMessage], blocking) enforces this: a message to a currently-ACTIVE teammate (non-terminal in the roster, or owning a present worktree) is allowed silently; a resume of a completed/removed teammate is BLOCKED by default. For a file-bearing follow-up, spawn a FRESH teammate with `isolation: "worktree"` under a new name. The one escape hatch is a live `resume-ack: <where any writes will land + why this resume is safe>` message line — for pure-question follow-ups or serialized external-repo writers — logged to `.claude/state/resume-acks.log`. The lead/reply channel (`to: main`/`team-lead`) and protocol messages (shutdown/plan-approval) are never blocked. **Name reuse is structurally impossible:** `guard-worktree-isolation.sh` also blocks any spawn whose `name` has EVER been used this session — active OR completed (union of a self-maintained `spawned-names.log` ledger + the roster + the idle/task event logs). No escape hatch — identifiers are free, so pick a fresh `<role>-<model>-<identifier>` (`dev-sonnet-1` → `dev-sonnet-2`/`dev-sonnet-b`); the `<model>` token must stay truthful to the model that instance boots on. Both guards read the session teams dir from `SESSION_TEAMS_DIR` in `orchestration.config` and take effect from the NEXT session (hooks are snapshotted per session).

- **Never infer an agent is finished from filesystem inactivity, a missing lock or the roster.** Finished is the platform's own end-of-run signal, or the session having ended (it recorded its end, or the operating system says its process is gone); a recorded pause, with what ends it, is not finished (`docs/plans/worktree-spec-2026-09-11.md`, points 11 and 12). `mega-lander/workspaces.sh status` shows every agent's state from that record. Background native-isolation agents don't appear in the SendMessage roster — absence ≠ finished.
- **Non-handoffs:** a completion notification with a stub result ("waiting on the run") is not a report — inspect the worktree, resume ONCE to commit+verify+report, then take over if it loops. **Corollary (zombie residue):** a background child (a detached long-running verification — device install, build, sync) can outlive BOTH its agent AND its worktree — after the worktree is reaped, the orphan re-creates the path and writes ghost state into it. So reap PROCESSES, not just directories: when you take over a stalled agent, identify a stray through its recorded launch context and stop only a process you can prove belongs to your assigned work or that the lead explicitly named as the target to quit; never choose a PID to kill by matching a name, path or worktree string. Prefer running long verification FOREGROUND so it dies with its agent. (Playbook #13; `detect-nonnative-worktree.sh` reports unregistered residue directories and orphaned PIDs; it deletes nothing.)
- **A workspace is deleted when its work is landed or discarded, and at no other time** (`docs/plans/worktree-spec-2026-09-11.md`). `mega-lander/workspaces.sh land <agent>` (its branches are already in main and nothing is uncommitted) or `mega-lander/workspaces.sh discard <agent> --reason '…'` stops the agent's processes and deletes every workspace and branch it has, as one; a deletion that fails is retried automatically. Finished work that is already merged is landed on its own at the turn end, and a turn cannot end while finished work is neither landed nor discarded. There is no sweep and no other deleter; `codex/` is never touched.
- **Tangle recovery:** shut down ALL suspects, wait for terminations, spawn ONE replacement under a NEW name on a FRESH worktree.
- **Engineers commit on their branch and report SHAs as their final step.** "Don't merge — Rich lands" does not mean "don't commit."

**Rich is the only writer to `main`.** Full serialized land sequence (one handoff at a time): `skills/rich-lander/SKILL.md` — verify committed branch vs MERGE-BASE, `git merge` from the main checkout (never inside a worktree), clean-state verify, push, deploy, `scripts/collect-worktree-artifacts.sh` before removal, then `mega-lander/workspaces.sh land <agent>`, which stops the agent's processes and deletes every workspace and branch it has. Engineers never merge into main, push, or deploy.

**Atomic commits are mandatory.** Every meaningful change = its own commit, immediately. Rich verifies commit count matches distinct changes before landing.

**Scratch that nobody is using is deleted on a schedule, and you never do it by hand.** `scripts/scratch-reaper.sh` (launchd, every six hours) deletes finished sessions' scratchpads, the temp workspaces killed harnesses leave behind, and nightly build products past the declared retention — each one only after PROVING nobody owns it, from the process table and the session registry rather than from an mtime, and each one written to `~/.claude/state/scratch-reaper.log` with the path, the bytes and why it qualified. Run it with no arguments to see the plan and delete nothing; `--apply` is the only mode that removes anything. A live session's scratch, a registered workspace and anything containing a `.git` are never touched — they are reported as UNDECIDABLE instead, which is the half of the output a person actually has to read. It exists because on 2026-09-17 a full disk was found by the operating system rather than by any of this.

**Garbage is ALWAYS cleaned up, or Rich gets a MASSIVE ALERT — CEO ruling, 2026-09-18 (`ceo-decisions` §54), after 105 GB appeared in a few hours and was still there the next morning.** The reaper above was installed and running that whole night; it fired either side of the incident and freed 156 KB, because its $TMPDIR coverage was an ALLOWLIST OF NAMES and a sandbox created by a bare `mktemp -d` matched none of them. An allowlist can only contain the names somebody thought of, so scratch is now ALLOCATED and not named:

- **`scripts/lib/scratch.sh`** is the only way engine code creates a scratch directory. One root (`$TMPDIR/richos-scratch`), one machine-wide ledger with the owning pid, and the sweeper predicts nothing — it reads the ledger and asks the process table. A child of that root with no ledger row is garbage BY CONSTRUCTION, because asking the allocator is the only way to get one. `scripts/scratch-allocation-lint.sh` refuses new code that goes around it.
- **`scripts/scratch-sweep.sh`** is ONE COMMAND, safe to call constantly and concurrently, printing one line of JSON on every exit path. The RichOS app calls it at boot, whenever an assignment settles, and on quit, because the CEO's words are *"The RichOS app must always clean up its garbage automatically, no matter what"*. A land calls it too. The launchd timer and the session hooks are BACKSTOPS, not the mechanism.
- **`scripts/disk-watchdog.sh`** is a SEPARATE mechanism with its own 15-minute timer, because his words are *"in case the disk storage gets close to running out for any reason"* — a watchdog wired into the cleanup can only see the space that cleanup knows about. It notifies the CEO on the machine itself at 50 GB and 25 GB free (`osascript`, which needs no app and no session), and it raises a **MASSIVE ALERT** to Rich at every session start and every turn end below 60 GB, on a 20 GB drop between readings, or on any sweep failure — repeated every turn until it clears, and it cannot be silenced by configuration, because an alert with an off switch is not an alert.
- **A deletion that FAILS is durable.** It is retried every run regardless of age and keeps alerting until the path is gone. That is not defensive coding: the first real sweep failed on five paths and the NEXT run reported `failures=0` with all five still on disk, because the partial `rmtree` had bumped each directory's mtime and the age floor then kept it silently. The reaper's own failure erased its own evidence.
- **When the alert says a path could not be deleted, Rich deletes it by hand.** That is the second half of the ruling and it is not optional: `scripts/disk-watchdog.sh --status` names the paths and how long each has been stuck.

**PAUSE IS NOT STOP. Rich sends exactly one standard pause message.** Generate its
SendMessage payload with `python3 <engine>/scripts/lib/pause_protocol.py --to <exact-agent-name>`
(for a quota hold add `--reason quota --reset HH:MMZ`). That command freezes the
agent itself BEFORE it prints anything (`HELD <agent>: ...`, or `NOT HELD` and exit 3),
and `--resume --to <agent>` releases it the same way, so a message Claude Code refuses
or loses never prevents or undoes a hold. Send the returned payload
unchanged. Do not write, append, summarize or "improve" pause instructions.
The quota watcher uses this same template; the terminal and desktop delivery
guards reject altered pause messages before delivery. In the desktop, use
`richos_work.pause_message` (and `richos_work.resume_message` to resume): the same
function freezes or releases the agent before the tool returns the same payload,
and its `hold` field says which. Pause preserves the same task,
context, workspaces and work in progress. Never substitute TaskStop, termination,
cancellation, hand-in, workspace removal or a replacement. In particular, never
add "end any heavy run" or "re-run what was cut off". A request sent is not proof
of a pause: check the actual hold and report a limitation honestly. Stop remains
a separate destructive operation under `guard-stop-live-work.sh`; a pause request
never authorizes it.

## Working Rules

1. **Processes are owned, never matched.** Never use `pkill -f`, `pgrep | kill` or `kill` on a PID chosen by matching a name, path or worktree string. Your own process and a wait loop can carry those strings in their arguments. Stop only a PID you captured at spawn, one explicitly handed to you by the lead as the target to quit or the owner of a port you opened. Confirm its recorded launch context belongs to the assigned work: its working directory is inside that workspace, or it runs in the disposable guest assigned to you. Test instances use isolated fixture data, never the developer's live install or live data. Before a heavy step, use the project's build-state mechanism where one exists. If name-based polling is necessary, exclude your own process tree and shell or wait wrappers, verify the remaining process identities and state how you checked. Process absence is not a reservation against another build starting.

2. **Every surface, with an applicability statement.** A change can work on the path you tested and be missing everywhere else. Before reporting, state which surfaces applied and what you checked: every entrance to the action, including buttons, menus, keyboard, voice and remote clients; affected clients, providers and shared wire contracts; light and dark themes; first-run and returning-user paths; reconnect behavior and reverse transitions; isolation of test data from live data. If you added a way in, add the way out and the way to see its state. A one-way door is a bug. Mark unrelated surfaces as not applicable rather than walking them without a reason.

3. **Smallest proof, then the required checks.** Run the tests covering the change, using the assigned repository's own proof selector when available from the assigned worktree. Use that worktree's copy when the selector derives the checkout from its own location. Include the owning suite of any screen you changed and run it twice back to back when a screenshot is involved. Do not run repo-wide checks unless the brief asks; required landing and release checks remain the lead's and the build's responsibility. Before starting work that needs a shared build lock, wait for the current build to release it and report how long you waited. Do not bypass the lock.

   **Retry unresolved failures first.** Before launching or resuming verification,
   inspect existing results and source identity. Test the failed, timed-out,
   refused or unrun unit before repeating unchanged passing work. Preserve
   optimized runner defaults and resource guards; do not silently reduce workers
   or shards or raise limits. Diagnose admission and timeout failures from their
   logs before changing execution settings. Before any broad rerun, record the
   specific invalidation or fresh-run requirement and why an isolated retry plus
   applicable receipts cannot close the gap. Use the existing receipt verifier
   where available; check source fingerprints and relevant inputs independently.
   Preserve superseded failures and receipt provenance. Never claim that a
   reconciled result was a single clean invocation. Stop once required evidence
   is complete. In RichOS repository work, read
   `docs/development/verification-retries.md` before running tests. Instructions
   alone are not a claim of mechanical enforcement.

4. **Working material.** Keep disposable scratch outside the worktree, in a directory named for you that only you write to: under the scratch root your lead's instructions declare, else `$TMPDIR/<your-name>/`. Never use the session scratchpad the harness advertises as yours: in a team session it belongs to the lead's session and every in-process teammate shares it, so nothing in it is yours to delete (`guard-shared-scratchpad.sh` refuses a teammate's delete there). Engine code uses the engine's registered scratch allocator. Do not introduce a competing allocation convention. Preserve useful results, then delete your own named directory, and only it, before reporting and show the cleanup command's result. Plans, research and private incident details belong in the private record repository, never a publication-bound repository; follow a brief that asks for a durable record there. A helper you needed and did not find in the committed toolkit is added to the toolkit and committed, never written as a throwaway.

contract-rules: 2026-09-20

### Handoff = the commit, not a message

The agent↔lead mailbox is ~50% lossy — **no load-bearing signal ever depends on it.** Durable substrates only: the git commit, the task store, hook-written event logs, the spawn prompt.

- A teammate's deliverable = its commits + marking its task complete. Rich detects completion via the `TeammateIdle` → `idle-events.jsonl` and `TaskCompleted` → `task-events.jsonl` hooks (session team dir), then reads GROUND TRUTH (`git log` on the branch, the diff) — never a message. A completed task is pruned from the queryable store; the durable record is the event log + the commit.
- Courtesy summaries (worktree, branch, SHAs oldest-first, touches) are welcome but advisory. No retry loops, no waiting for acks.
- Mid-work corrections go in the teammate's TASK (durable, bidirectional) or a fresh re-spawn with the corrected brief — never a load-bearing follow-up message.
- `ScheduleWakeup` is the safety-net fallback so a dropped notification can't dead-idle the session; hooks + ground truth are primary.

### Deployment — ZERO TOLERANCE FOR GAPS

**Deploy-always: what's on main MUST be what's on staging — no exceptions, including docs-only commits** (a short no-op deploy is cheaper than a stale-staging QA failure). Deploy is the final step of Rich's land sequence. Engineers never deploy.

